"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nRowe, who had been idly watching Pechins doodle, stood up from his chair, and Pechins and Lane looked at him curiously. [2060] \"What are you writing?\" the young man asked sharply. [2061] \"What?\" Percy blinked. \"Just the symbol 3HS wM that's been driving us crazy!\" [2062] \"Hurrah!\" Roe exclaimed, jumping up and his eyes shining. \"I know! I know! It's so simple and childish!\" [2063] Jules Reine stood up, walked to the table, and his face was illuminated by the light. He said, \"You finally figured it out, Percy. When we sat in your father's office and he opened the Saxon letter to see what was written inside, I saw it and understood it too. Gordon, tell her.\" [2064] \"I don't understand what you two are talking about,\" Percy complained. [2065] \"How was I sitting when you wrote the symbol?\" Roe asked. [2066] \"Right in front of the table, facing me!\" [2067] \"Absolutely right. In other words, I saw this symbol just like Mr. Renet saw it when the commissioner opened the original letter. He saw what I saw - I saw it backwards!\" [2068] Percy gasped. She picked up the paper and turned it over. The symbol now looked like this:\n[2069] She slowly repeated, \"Wm She.\" Each letter seemed to have a different taste in her mouth, \"This looks like a signature. W-m...William...\" - both men looked at her intently - \"...William Shakespeare (1)!\" She shouted, jumping up from her chair, \"William Shakespeare!\" [2070] After a while, Percy sat down on the carpet next to the old gentleman's feet, and his pale, slender fingers played with her hair. Rowley relaxed comfortably in front of them. [2071] \"Ever since then, I've been thinking about this over and over in my mind,\" Reen explained wearily, \"When I analyzed it, it seemed quite clear. Dr. All\u00e8s was not imitating Shakespeare's signature; this copy should be the handwriting of the Elizabethan era. He simply wrote it in his own way - perhaps thinking he had written it clearly - the uppercase letters in this unusual Shakespeare signature. Unusual because of the lowercase m and the handwritten e. But why is H capitalized? It could be All\u00e8s' peculiar behavior, but it doesn't matter. What matters is that -\"\n[2072] \"It's one of Shakespeare's signatures,\" Rowley said slowly, \"It's quite strange!\" [2073] Reen sighed. \"Gordon, you know better than me that there are only six genuine Shakespeare signatures in the world.\" [2074] \"Strangely enough,\" the young man said, \"one of them is written as Will Shak'P'.\" [2075] \"Yes! There are also some so-called 'suspicious' signatures, one of which has a spelling like All\u00e8s' symbol - a capital W, a lowercase m connected to the top of the W, then a capital S, a lowercase h, and a lowercase e that is also connected.\" [2076] \"Is it like the old English script of 'ye'?\" Percy asked. [2077] \"You got it right. This suspicious signature appears in the Metamorphoses of Ovid (2) by Aldine, which is now housed in the Bodleian Library at Oxford.\" [2078] \"I saw it when I was in England,\" Rowe said. [2079] The old man continued, \"I checked with the Bodleian Library, and the book of Ovid is still there. I thought the whole thing might be related to the theft of that book. Of course, that's a ridiculous idea.\" [2080] \"You mean -\" Percy's tone was one of awe, \"that all these thefts, doubts, and everything else are related to the discovery of Shakespeare's seventh true signature?\" [2081] \"It seems so, doesn't it?\" Rowe said with a bitter smile. \"I've wasted my youth here. Ha, ha! I've never come across anything as momentous as this while going through Elizabethan records.\" [2082] \"What else could it be?\" Raine whispered. \"If the secret is really worth a million, Dr. All\u00e8s had reason to believe the signature was genuine. Otherwise, how could it be worth a million? Ha, it's a very interesting question.\" [2083] The young man murmured softly, \"If it has incalculable historical and literary value, it is priceless in itself.\" [2084] \"I read somewhere that a completely authentic seventh signature of Shakespeare found at an auction was worth a million pounds. I don't know if it's pounds or dollars! But the signature must have a purpose; it is usually associated with some document.\" [2085] \"The paper in the book!\" Percy exclaimed. [2086] \"Shh, Petty. It's not necessarily so,\" Rowe pondered. \"Six real signatures, of course, are associated with certain documents: one on a legal document where the old man was involved in a lawsuit; one on a contract signed when buying a house around 1612; another when paying for the same house; and the last three on his three wills. But it could also be on the back of a book's endpapers, you know.\" [2087] \"I doubt it,\" Raine shook his head. \"As Percy has already noticed, the seventh signature might be on a document - a transaction document, a lease - where the document itself has little historical value? Hmm, maybe\u2026\"\n[2088] \"The value can't be small,\" Rowe objected. \"If it's a transaction document or a lease, it could be of great importance, possibly explaining where Shakespeare was at some point - to clarify many things.\" [2089] \"Yes, yes. I said the value was small from a human perspective. But what if it's on a letter?\" Raine leaned forward, his fingers tightening in Percy's hair, making her almost cry out, \"Think of the possibilities! A signed letter by the immortal Shakespeare!\" [2090] \"I was thinking -\" Rowe muttered. \"This is too much. Who is it for? What does it say? Autobiographical data. A genuine Shakespeare manuscript.\" [2091] \"Of course, it's within the realm of possibility,\" the old gentleman's voice sounded slightly strained, \"If the signature appears at the end of the letter, the letter would be more valuable than the signature! No wonder the scholars are constantly scheming. It's like - oh, finding the actual manuscript of Saint Paul's letters!\" [2092] \"The document was in the 1599 Jagger,\" Perkins whispered hoarsely, \"Dr. Aless discovered the other two existing 1599 Jagger volumes and couldn't find anything, so he resorted to obtaining the third one, which belonged to the collection of Saxen. And he found it! This - is it possible...\"\n[2093] \"It seems so,\" Rowe smiled, \"He found it, lucky man!\" [2094] \"But now it's stolen. Oh, dear, I'd bet it was in Dr. Aless' cabinet.\" [2095] \"It's quite possible,\" said Reane. \"One more thing. I found out that the stolen and returned third book was actually bought by Samuel Saxen from Sir Humphrey, a collector from England.\" [2096] \"The same person?\" Percy was startled. [2097] \"The same.\" Raine shrugged, \"Sir Humphrey died just a few weeks ago. No, no. Don't be alarmed. His death was natural, no foul play. As usual, God was the executioner. He was eighty-nine and died of pneumonia complications. My contact on the other side told me that the Jager Saxon bought from Sir Humphrey was the one that caused all the trouble, and it had belonged to the Humphrey family since the Elizabethan era. Sir Humphrey was the last surviving member of the family, with no heirs.\" [2098] \"He couldn't have known the document was hidden in the book's cover,\" Rowe said, \"otherwise he wouldn't have sold it.\" [2099] \"Of course not. In fact, several generations of the Humphrey family didn't know or suspect the existence of the document.\" [2100] Percy asked, \"But why was the document hidden in the book cover? Who did it?\" [2101] Rine sighed, \"That's a question. I think the document has been hidden there for hundreds of years, probably written for a contemporary, who knows? But the document needed to be hidden, and I believe it has a special value or meaning in itself.\" [2102] Old Quincy slipped into the study, his wrinkled face resembling a leaf, with no part of it untouched by wrinkles. He tugged at the owner's sleeve and complained, \"Mr. Jury, there's a man named Bowlin, a police officer from Tarrytown.\" [2103] Rine frowned, \"You old knave! What did you say?\" [2104] \"He called. He told me that just an hour ago, at seven o'clock\" - the study clock pointed to seven - \"Aless's house was mysteriously blown up!\" [2105] --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- --- ---\n[2106] (1) The name of William Shakespeare. [2107] (2) Ovid (43 BC-17 AD), Roman poet. [2108] (3) Caliban, a half-human, half-beast monster in Shakespeare's play \"The Tempest.\" Raine used it as a nickname for Quixie. [2109] Chapter Twenty-Four\n[2110] Catastrophe and Discovery\n[2111] The house became a smoky ruin ravaged by fire. Thick yellow smoke still hung on the nearby charred branches, and the air was filled with a choking smell of sulfur. The old wooden buildings were reduced to rubble, and fragments of walls and roofs littered the roads. The house collapsed on top of the cellar, and smoke still rose from the ash-covered ground. State troopers pushed their way through the curious crowd gathering around. The firefighters of Tarrytown had brought the fire under control and were now focusing on preventing the fire from spreading to the dry wood. The water supply for the fire engines was insufficient, so additional water tanks had to be brought in from Tarrytown and Ewington. [2112] Officer Pauling met Perkins, Rowe, and Rayne at the edge of the open space. His ruddy face was covered in soot, and he was panting heavily. He roared, \"Damn it! My two men are badly injured. Luckily, no one was inside when it happened. It exploded at six o'clock.\" [2113] \"No warning?\" Rayne asked, showing signs of excitement, \"I don't think it was a bomb dropped from a plane, right?\" [2114] \"Impossible. There hasn't been a single plane flying overhead all day. My two men said that no one came near the house for an hour or two after we left.\" [2115] \"So it must have been a bomb placed inside the house beforehand,\" Rowe said gloomily. \"Thank goodness it didn't go off earlier. What a close call!\" [2116] \"We could have been here when it exploded\u2014\" Perkins' face turned pale, \"This\u2014this is hard to believe. A bomb!\" [2117] \"It could have been in the cellar,\" Lane said indifferently, \"We didn't search there this afternoon. What an idiotic oversight!\" [2118] \"The cellar\u2014I suppose that's where it was,\" Bowlin grumbled, \"Well, my men need to be taken to the hospital. I'll go check on them. They were almost blown to pieces. We'll come back tomorrow when the fire is completely out.\" [2119] The three sat quietly in the old gentleman's car on the way back to Hamlet Manor, lost in their own thoughts. Rowe seemed particularly absorbed, with his fingers on his lower lip and his gaze fixed on the sky. [2120] \"You know,\" Rowe suddenly spoke up, \"I was thinking\u2014\"\n[2121] \"What?\" Perkins said. [2122] \"It seems like there's a group of people involved in this. Without a doubt, that Shakespeare document, whatever it is, must be the key. I think Dr. Alessi found it in the Jagger of 1599 that he stole from the museum, making him one of the main players. The other one is the guy who was waving an axe around last night, if he wasn't after the document, what was he? That's point two. Another person arrived after the ax-wielder, and he didn't close the secret cabinet door, that's point three. Now there's this explosion, someone planted a bomb, that's point four, God, that's enough to give you a headache.\" [2123] \"Maybe not.\" Perkins argued, \"The main players you mentioned - you're so technical - could be the same person. The second person to enter the house could have been Dr. Alessi, making the number three. The ax-wielder could have been the one who planted the bomb, leaving only two - Gordon, I'm going on like this, but there's one thing that's important. Now that I have time to think about this awful explosion, I have a very strange idea.\" [2124] The fog in Reine's eyes cleared, replaced by a look of curiosity. [2125] \"We've been assuming that the person chasing the document wanted the document itself - stealing, owning, preserving it, all for the money - a common criminal motive.\" [2126] Rowe chuckled. \"Patty, you talk fresh! Of course, the motivation for digging up something valuable is usually like that, and that's a normal explanation.\" [2127] Pechin sighed. \"Maybe I'm crazy, but I can't help thinking that if the bomb was placed last night, the person who put it there probably knew the document was in the house!\" [2128] The old gentleman blinked. \"Is that so, Pechin?\" [2129] \"Oh, I think it's crazy, but we're dealing with extreme acts of violence - gunshots, theft, explosions... Only Maxwell was in the house, so the person who set the bomb must have known that. If you think the target of that bomb was the harmless old servant, that's absurd. If it wasn't him, who or what was it? We always thought the person or people chasing the document would keep it. Tell you, someone's goal was to destroy it!\" [2130] Rowe paused for a moment, then threw his head back and bit his teeth. \"Oh, Patty, you're my nemesis. When it comes to women's arguments...\" He rubbed his eyes. \"What kind of bad guy wants to destroy a document with such a historical and monetary value? Who's so crazy!\" [2131] Pensin blushed. \"I think you're annoying.\" [2132] \"Gordon, Pensin's reasoning is very logical.\" Ryan quickly chimed in, \"Child, you can't outsmart this lady. I think if the document had only Shakespeare's signature, only a madman would destroy it. But if there's more to it than just the signature, the person who put the bomb definitely doesn't want the public to know what's on it, whatever it is.\" [2133] \"Well, what do you think, self-proclaimed genius?\" Pensin said. [2134] \"But to destroy -\" Luo Wei frowned, \"I can't imagine what secret Shakespeare would write that would make people in the 20th century go to great lengths to cover up. What is it? It doesn't make sense!\" [2135] \"That's the point.\" Ryan said coldly, \"What is it? If you know - as for whether it makes sense or not, that's another matter.\" [2136] If asked about Pechin, she might say that after this day, nothing would surprise her anymore. Looking back, the day started with a strange phone call, followed by a series of strange events \u2013 an old man attacked, a house mysteriously destroyed, and ended with a terrible explosion. Little did she know, there were still things waiting for her, Roe, and Raine at Hamlet Manor. [2137] As the sky darkened, the drawbridge was lit up, and the old face of Quincy shone brightly in front of the ancient lanterns. [2138] \"Mr. Jules!\" he called. \"Is anyone hurt?\" [2139] \"Not seriously, Quincy. What's going on?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nSo the second person must have been the victim; he didn't leave the house because Maxwell didn't hear the doorbell warning again, and all the doors and windows were locked from the inside. But if he was the victim, and he was alone in the house, who killed him? No, like Gordon said, the one who left the house was the murderer, and the murderer was the masked man.\" [2430] \"What does this lead to?\" Lane asked slowly. [2431] \"The murderer!\" [2432] \"Yes!\" Rowe agreed. [2433] \"I'll tell you\u2014don't talk, Gordon! There were two people in the house that night. One of them was the victim, one of the Seldrake brothers\u2014the dead man couldn't be made up like that. Well, one of them walked into the house and knew exactly where the files were\u2014he went straight to the secret cabinet in the study. The other didn't know, and he almost wrecked the whole house looking for it. Well, who's most likely to know where the hiding place is?\" [2434] \"That Alec fellow\u2014William Seldrake,\" the Inspector answered. [2435] \"Right, Dad. Because the hiding place was set up by him, and the stuff was stowed away by him. Since the second visitor knew the hiding place\u2014the first one, the axeman, didn't\u2014so Alec is the second person. The fact that the second person easily entered the house confirms this. The door always closes automatically, Maxwell's duplicate key was taken by the first person, but the second person could still get in, using Alec's original key, isn't it?\" [2436] \"Who do you think the masked man was?\" her father asked. [2437] \"There's evidence for that too. We found fragments of a single lens in the hallway. Dr. Sedra is the only person involved in the case who wears a single lens. Maxwell never saw a single lens in the house. This means that Hamnett Sedra was in the house on the night of the murder! If Hamnett was in the house, then he's one of the two, the other being his brother William - Dr. Alec. But since William was the victim, as I said, Hamnett must have been the one who killed his own brother!\" [2438] \"Damn!\" Sam said. [2439] \"No, no, Percy,\" Roarke jumped up, \"it's -\"\n[2440] \"Wait, Gordon,\" Reine quietly said, \"Percy, what makes you think Dr. Hamnett Sedra is the main character in this case?\" [2441] Percy glared angrily at Rowe. \"I have several reasons why Dr. Hamnett Sedgwick would want to get his hands on Shakespeare's manuscript. First, he is a bibliophile, and he admits that William told him everything about the manuscript. I dare say he is a typical bookworm and would not let the opportunity to see Shakespeare's true work slip away. Another suspicious point is that he suddenly gave up his position as the curator of the London Kingston Museum and accepted a similar position in the United States, which he hated, and with a lower salary - coincidentally, this allowed him to conveniently contact the J\u00e4ger of Saxony! Finally, he secretly arrived in New York before the scheduled date.\" [2442] Reine sighed: \"Percy, you are truly brilliant.\" [2443] \"And -\" Percy continued enthusiastically, \"Hamnett being the axeman is based on the fact that only he does not know where the manuscript is hidden, so he must slash blindly, like the real act of the one with the axe... It is easy to recreate the scene with two Sedgwick in the house. Hamnett was slashing blindly in William's bedroom upstairs when William came in, took the things from the hidden place in the study. They met soon after, and Hamnett saw the document in William's hand, so he swung the axe at his watch and wrist. During the struggle, Hamnett's single lens fell and broke. Hamnett shot William, dragged the body into -\"\n[2444] \"No!\" Rowe shouted, \"Percy, shut up. Mr. Reine, listen to me. Everything I just said can be agreed upon to some extent - William and Hamnett are the two people in the house, William is the one who took the document, and Hamnett is the masked man and the axeman. But it was not William who was killed by Hamnett, but Hamnett who was killed by William! The corpse in the rubble could be either of them. I believe that the person who called himself Hamnett and was almost starving when we found him in the house is actually William!\" [2445] \"Gordon,\" Percy countered, \"that - that is stupid. You forgot that the original key to the house was found on the corpse, which proves that the corpse is William's.\" [2446] \"Ah, no, Perkins,\" Lane interjected, \"that doesn't make sense logically. Gordon, go on. Why do you think this clever argument is wrong?\" [2447] \"Based on psychology, sir. I admit it's hard to find evidence to support my point of view. I believe the person lying in the hospital bed lied about his identity because, as William Sedley, he was a wanted fugitive by the French police; as a survivor, he now has the documents and wants to dispose of them freely. Don't forget that he knows all about it and can say things that are in his favor. The inspector and the reporter talked the night before, and all the details were published in the newspaper, and the rest he could find out the next morning from the reporter.\" [2448] Lane chuckled oddly. \"Gordon, I agree theoretically that this motive could be established, and this argument is very clever. But who placed the bomb?\" [2449] Perkins and Rowe exchanged glances. Then both hastily agreed that the bomb was placed by a third party twenty-four hours before the murder, with the sole purpose of destroying the documents, for reasons unknown. After placing the bomb, the third party thought his work was done and disappeared from the scene. [2450] The elderly gentleman grumbled. \"But what about the kidnapping? Why did the survivor \u2013 whether it was William or Hamnet \u2013 deliberately get involved in this mess and then be helplessly discovered by the police? Do you remember how exhausted and hungry he was when we found him?\" [2451] \"That's easy,\" Percy retorted, \"whether it's William or Hamnett, their motives are the same: to frame the deceased for the fake kidnapping, thus strengthening the apparent innocence of the conspirator.\" [2452] Rowe nodded, somewhat doubtfully. [2453] \"What about Donohue?\" asked the inspector. [2454] \"If Hamnett is the survivor,\" answered Percy, \"then he is the one who kidnapped Donohue because Donohue saw him leaving the Alley's house and thought he was a co-conspirator with William. He may have thought that by kidnapping Donohue, he could get the location of the hidden documents from him\u2014remember he threatened to torture him?\" [2455] \"But if William is the survivor\u2026\" Rowe pointed out sharply, \"then it was he who kidnapped Donohue because Donohue was following him and might have threatened his plan.\" [2456] \"So now the question is,\" Reen continued, \"you both agree that Hamnet Sedley and William Sedley are involved in this crime, but you disagree on who killed whom. I must say, that's quite a predicament!\" [2457] \"Good heavens!\" the sergeant suddenly exclaimed, his eyes bulging, \"this couldn't have come at a worse time!\" [2458] \"Dad, what do you mean?\" Petty asked. [2459] \"Percy, before you came back, Reen told us that he believes this British man may have been lying about his identity, and we have a way to test if he's telling the truth!\" [2460] \"A way to test?\" Percy furrowed his brow, \"I can't think of one.\" [2461] \"It's actually quite simple,\" Rene said as he stood up. \"We need to go to Britain. Gordon, did you leave the man who claims to be Hamnet Sedley there?\" [2462] \"Yes, sir.\" [2463] \"Excellent. Let's go. This should only take five minutes to resolve.\" [2464] Chapter 29\n[2465] Visual Illusion\n[2466] They found the person who called himself Hamnet Sedley working with Dr. Jott in the library director's office. The director was somewhat surprised when they walked in, but the British man quickly stood up and smiled over to them. His pleasant demeanor faded as he saw their serious expressions. [2467] \"It's a pleasure to see you,\" he said in a friendly tone, but the smile disappeared when he saw their stern faces. \"I hope there's no trouble.\" [2468] \"We all hope so.\" The sergeant said tersely, \"Dr. Jott, would you mind if we speak with Dr. Sedley privately? It's a matter of great importance.\" [2469] \"Importance?\" The director had risen from his seat and stood still, looking at each of them before lowering his head and rummaging through some papers. \"Oh\u2014of course.\" A blush slowly rose on his face. He moved around the desk and quickly left the room. Dr. Sedley remained still, and there was silence in the room for a moment. Then Sam nodded to Raine, who took a step forward. [2470] \"Dr. Sedley,\" Raine's face showed no emotion, \"For...let's say, for scientific interest, we must conduct a very simple test on you...Percy, lend me your briefcase for a moment.\" [2471] \"A test?\" The Englishman's face showed displeasure, and he put his hand in his coat pocket. [2472] Bishop quickly handed the handbag to Rain. He opened the handbag, looked inside, took out a brightly colored handkerchief, and then closed the handbag. \"Very well, sir, please tell me, what color is this handkerchief?\" [2473] Bishop held his breath, his eyes widening with some realization, while the others stared blankly at the scene. [2474] Dr. Sedley blushed, a complex expression surging on his hawk-like face. He took a step back and said sternly, \"This is ridiculous. What is the purpose of this childish game?\" [2475] Rain whispered, \"Of course, just to confirm the innocent color of this little handkerchief, no harm, right?\" [2476] There was a silence. Then the British man spoke without turning around, his tone stiff, \"Blue.\" - The handkerchief had green, yellow, and white colors. [2477] \"Dr. Sedra's tie, Reine?\" Reine continued to ask, his expression unchanged. [2478] The British man swayed, his eyes pained. \"Brown.\" - It was actually sapphire blue. [2479] \"Thank you.\" Reine returned the handkerchief and briefcase to Perkins. \"Inspector, this man is not Dr. Hamnet Sedra, he is William Sedra, sometimes called Dr. Alessi.\" [2480] The British man suddenly collapsed into a chair, burying his face in his hands. [2481] \"God, how did you know?\" Sam whispered. [2482] Raine sighed. \"Inspector, this is basic work. On May 6th, Dr. Alessi, or William Sedra, went to your office and entrusted you with that envelope. That person could not be Dr. Hamnett Sedra, as he himself said. Dr. Hamnett Sedra was in London on May 7th for a reception in his honor. The man with the envelope, of course, was the one who wrote the symbols inside - he admitted as much in your office that morning. What does the paper and symbols represent?\" [2483] \"Ah, just... damn, I don't know.\" The inspector said. [2484] Raine tiredly said: \"The paper was a light gray, with the heading 'Saxony Library' in a deeper gray. This and the form of the symbols immediately reminded me.\" [2485] \"What does it mean? We just made a mistake. You were lucky, you just guessed right.\" [2486] \"That's right. In other words, William Sedra wrote Wm SHe upside down on the paper. To correctly read those symbols, one must turn the paper upside down, which is very unusual. Someone picks up a paper with a heading, intending to write on it, and instinctively puts the paper right side up \u2013 that is, with the name and address at the top. However, the person who wrote these symbols took the paper and wrote it upside down! Why?\" [2487] \"I see.\" Percy sighed. \"Unless it was an accident, he couldn't see the printed markings!\" [2488] \"Yes, dear, that's exactly it. It seems impossible, but it's more likely that Dr. Aless accidentally turned the paper over and wrote a few letters, not realizing it would make any difference to the person who saw the symbols later. However, another possibility is logically possible, and I cannot ignore it. I asked myself: if it were true, what kind of miracle could this phenomenon create? Why didn't Dr. Aless see the deep gray print on the Saxon paper? Was he blind? But that's hard to believe. The person who came to your office seemed to have good eyesight. Then I remembered something else, and suddenly saw the answer... the mustache.\" [2489] The British man lifted his pain-filled eyes, now filled with curiosity. He murmured, \"Mustache?\" [2490] \"You know,\" Reine smiled, \"he still doesn't understand what's wrong with the fake mustache he's wearing! Mr. Sedra, the mustache you wore that day was terrifying! It was a shocking blue-green, and who knows what other colors!\" [2491] Sedgwick opened his mouth wide, groaning, \"Oh, I bought it at a costume shop. I guess I didn't explain it well enough. The shopkeeper thought I wanted a... a ridiculous mustache for some crazy thing like dressing up as a ghost...\u201d\n[2492] \"Unfortunately,\" Renault said coldly, \"but the mustache and the note match. I think the person who wrote the symbol is very likely to be completely colorblind. I've heard of such cases, so I asked Dr. Martini. He told me that complete colorblindness is extremely rare. But when it happens, such a person sees all colors as different shades of gray, like a pencil sketch. He also mentioned another possibility - that he may not be completely colorblind, but has no color perception. That's better than being colorblind, but he can't see the differences in brightness of printed colors. When Dr. Martini checked the Saxon library note, he was quite sure that the person who wrote the symbol was affected by this visual defect.\" [2493] The British man moved, growled, \"I've never seen a color.\" [2494] They were silent for a moment. Renault sighed and continued, \"So I believe in my heart that Dr. Alessi is colorblind. Sir, you were just tormented by the same situation. You guessed the colors of Miss Sam's handkerchief and Mr. Rowe's tie, not knowing the real colors. Now you call yourself Hamnett Sedgwick, but Hamnett Sedgwick is not colorblind! We first saw him in this museum's Saxon room. He checked the repaired cabinet - the one with the stolen 1599 Jagger - and correctly identified the colors of the book covers in it, as well as the different shades of the same color. He could distinguish that a book cover was golden brown, which a colorblind person could not distinguish such subtle differences. Since you are either William or Hamnett, and Hamnett's vision is normal, while William is colorblind - you are colorblind, obviously you are William, and this reasoning is very simple. I proposed this test to see if you were lying. You did lie. Most of the story you told us in the hospital was fabricated, although I suspect there were some truths. Now please tell the whole story to my friend.\" [2495] He sat down in a chair and wiped his lips again. [2496] \"Okay,\" the British man's voice was low, \"I am William Zedra.\" [2497] When he first visited the inspector, he was Dr. Aless. He gave the letter with the symbol to the inspector; in case something happened to him while he was searching for Shakespeare's documents, it could serve as a clue - at the time, he thought the likelihood of something happening was very small. On June 20th, he didn't call because he couldn't, the very small possibility had occurred. His brother Hamnett - William found out then - accepted the position of director of the British Museum, with the sole purpose of approaching the 1599 Jagger in the Saxon collection. William stole the Jagger from the museum the night he did, and then Hamnett kidnapped William, which happened shortly after Donohue appeared, on the same night Donohue was also kidnapped by Hamnett. The Irishman had been unconscious for a while, so his sense of time was distorted... from William stealing the Jagger to the police rescuing him from the abandoned house, he was a prisoner. [2498] No matter how Hamnett threatened him, William refused to reveal the location of the documents. Of course, Donohue didn't know anything, and couldn't tell Hamnett anything. Hamnett, in order to go to the museum to work and pretend to be innocent, came and went to the place of confinement in a hurry, and couldn't do it regularly, so he became desperate. One day, he told William that he knew the documents were in William's house, and he put a bomb in the cellar to blow up the house and the documents! The bomb was made by a black market chemist on his own. It was then that William realized that his brother's true intention was not to preserve the Shakespeare documents, but to destroy them! [2499] \"But why?\" Rowe raised his fist and roared, \"That's... that's the most barbaric act of destruction! Oh, why destroy it?\" [2500] \"Is your brother insane?\" Percy exclaimed. [2501] The British man clenched his lips, looking at Reine, who was calmly looking into the distance. \"I don't know,\" he said. [2502] Hamnet set the timer on the bomb for twenty-four hours later. William realized that if he let the bomb go off, the documents would be destroyed and forever lost. He had to give up resistance, knowing that any delay would be more advantageous. He might be able to untie himself and save the documents. So he told Hamnet the location and method of opening the secret cabinet, but he couldn't escape. Hamnet proudly told him that he would go back to William's house and destroy the documents himself, and that he had plenty of time. He would remove the bomb's safety pin\u2026 Hamnet took William's key\u2014the original one\u2014and William never saw him alive again.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2140] \"There's someone in the hall who wants to see you. He called as soon as you left. He seemed very upset, Mr. Jules.\" [2141] \"Who is it?\" [2142] \"He said his name is Jot.\" [2143] They hurried towards the hall. The entire manor was designed to resemble a medieval British castle, and the layout of the hall echoed the past. Standing at a distance, with his hands behind his back, was the director of the British Museum, Dr. Jot, pacing under the tragic mask that Rowe had carefully placed at one end of the hall. [2144] The three of them rushed towards him. [2145] \"Dr. Jot,\" Rowe said slowly, \"I apologize for keeping you waiting. There were some unexpected events... Your face looks as grim as that mask. What's the matter?\" [2146] \"Unexpected events?\" Dr. Jotter said angrily. \"So you know about it?\" He nodded reluctantly at Percy and Rowe. [2147] \"The explosion?\" [2148] \"Explosion? What explosion? Oh, no! I mean Dr. Sedra.\" [2149] \"Dr. Sedra!\" The three of them exclaimed. [2150] \"He's missing.\" [2151] The librarian leans against an oak table, with bloodshot eyes. [2152] \"Missing?\" Percy furrows his brow, \"How could that be? We saw him on Saturday, didn't we, Gordon?\" [2153] \"Yes, indeed.\" The librarian rumbles, \"He came in on Saturday morning and stayed for a few minutes, seemed fine. Before he left, I asked him to call me at home on Sunday \u2013 last night \u2013 to discuss some museum matters. He agreed and left.\" [2154] \"He didn't call?\" Raine whispers. [2155] \"No. I contacted the Seneca Inn where he was staying, and he wasn't there. I waited for him all day today, hoping he'd get in touch, but nothing. Dr. Jotter shrugs, \"It's \u2013 it's stupid! He didn't say he was leaving. I thought maybe he was sick, so I called the inn again this afternoon. They said they haven't seen him since Saturday morning.\" [2156] \"That doesn't mean he disappeared Saturday!\" Rowe countered. [2157] \"I know. It's just strange. I don't know what to do. Should I call the police or\u2026 I tried to reach your father, Miss Samuels, but the office secretary said\u2026\"\n[2158] \"First Donoho, then Dr. Aless, and now Sedra,\" Percy lamented. \"These people are disappearing one by one! It's \u2013 it's unacceptable!\" [2159] \"Unless Sedra is Aless,\" Rowe said. [2160] Dr. Jotter ran his fingers through his hair. \"Oh, my God!\" [2161] Pinchot frowned. \"I don't think that necessarily means Dr. Aless is Sedra, or that he escaped with the file.\" [2162] \"Dear Miss Sampson, the hotel staff said all of his things were still in the room. I don't think a person running away would do that. What file are you talking about\u2014\"\n[2163] Raine looked exhausted, with deep circles under his eyes and skin that looked like crumpled parchment. He wearily shook his head: \"These conjectures won't get us anywhere. It's a surprising turn of events\u2026 The only thing I can suggest is to find out what happened to Sedra.\" [2164] Pinchot and Roebling arrived in town late at night. They parked their car outside the Seneca Hotel and went inside to find the manager. After some hesitation, they were granted permission to look at Dr. Sedra's room. The room seemed tidy, with British-cut clothes neatly hanging in the closet, freshly laundered sheets and pillowcases in the dresser, and three unopened suitcases and bags. The manager seemed reluctant to involve the police, glancing at Pinchot's credentials\u2014which, of course, were the detective's\u2014and reluctantly allowing the search of the room. [2165] The luggage and clothes were all British. There were some letters in the room, with postmarks from \"London\" and addressed to Dr. Hamnett Sedra, apparently from former British colleagues. The passport and visa were in order, issued to Dr. Hamnett Sedra, with a familiar-looking photo attached. [2166] \"It's Sedra, no doubt,\" Rowe said grimly. \"I'm starting to get paranoid about this. There's no sign here that this person wanted to flee the country.\" [2167] \"Ugh!\" Peachins muttered under his breath. \"Gordon, take me home, and\u2014and, kiss me!\" [2168] Chapter 25\n[2169] Murder\n[2170] The sun shone brightly. The fire had been extinguished, and the smoke had dispersed with the night, leaving only burnt-out ashes, piles of rubble, and charred trees to bear witness to the explosion of the night before. Firefighters and police were busy digging through the rubble. A dark-skinned man calmly directed the entire operation, his eyes sharp and eager to clear away the debris so he could descend into the cellar to see what was inside. [2171] They stood by the edge of the woods, the warm morning breeze rustling their clothes. Pauling watched the workers with a cold eye. [2172] \"See that guy with the hawk eyes? He's an explosives expert. I think since he's involved, he should do a good job! I want to see how this strange thing happened.\" [2173] \"You mean he'll find something in that pile of garbage?\" Roeve asked. [2174] \"That's why he's here.\" [2175] The workers made rapid progress, quickly clearing the debris blocking the cave. They shoveled the garbage to thirty feet away, hand over hand. Once the cellar was almost clear enough to descend, the silent man crawled into the hole and disappeared. Ten minutes later, he reappeared, looking around as if measuring the extent of the explosion, and then disappeared again, this time into the woods. He reappeared again and descended into the cellar. The third time he emerged, he wore a satisfied expression, holding in his hands a jumbled mess of iron pieces, rubber, glass, and wires. [2176] \"How's it going?\" Pauling asked. [2177] \"Sir, the evidence is here.\" The explosives expert said, picking up a fragment of a clock-like instrument, \"A time bomb.\" [2178] \"Ah!\" Mr. Philo Vance said. [2179] \"Primitive, homemade. Set to go off at six o'clock with yellow dynamite.\" [2180] Percy, Roe, and Vance all thought the same thing, but Vance was the first to ask: \"When was the bomb placed?\" [2181] \"At six o'clock on Sunday evening - if the bomb went off last night at six o'clock. It's a twenty-four-hour timer.\" [2182] \"Six o'clock on Sunday evening.\" Perkins repeated slowly, \"So it was put on before Maxwell was attacked on Sunday night!\" [2183] \"You're right, Perri.\" Rowe said, \"If the person who put the bomb knew there were documents in the house, his purpose was to destroy the documents, so he knew the stuff was in the house but didn't know exactly where. It's difficult -\"\n[2184] \"The epicenter of the explosion was the cellar.\" The expert spat on the charred rock. [2185] Reine let out another \"Ah.\" [2186] \"The second visitor \u2013 the one who took the documents out of the secret cabinet \u2013\" Perkins looked pointedly at Reen, \"could not have been the one who planted the bomb. It's obvious. The second visitor knew where the documents were; the bomber didn't, as you said, Gordon\u2026\" Perkins' words were interrupted by a coarse roar, coming from the workers digging through the cellar debris. They all turned around. [2187] \"What is it?\" Bowlin called, running over quickly. [2188] Three people were bending over something, their heads almost touching. One of them turned around, pale and trembling. He managed to speak: \"Sir, there's a body here. From the look on his face, he was \u2013 murdered.\" [2189] The young man ran towards the edge of the house foundation, stepping on black ash. Reen followed slowly, his face pale and anxious. [2190] Roarke looked at the scene in front of him and roughly pushed Perkins aside. \"Petty, get away from here,\" he said hoarsely. \"You'd better go stand under that tree over there. This \u2013 it's not good.\" [2191] \"Oh,\" Perkins said. Her nostrils flared, and she silently obeyed his request. [2192] The men all widened their eyes and focused intently on the deep hole. A young, red-faced policeman crawled out from the corner of the cellar, bent over, and began to shake and vomit... The body was badly burned, almost unrecognizable - one leg and one arm were missing, and the clothes were completely burnt. [2193] \"How do you know he was murdered?\" Reen asked sternly. [2194] An older policeman's mouth twisted: \"He's not completely burnt, I can see the hole.\" [2195] \"Hole?\" Rowe croaked. [2196] The man sighed. \"Three holes. In his stomach, clean and neat. Those are bullet holes, sir, don't forget.\" [2197] Three hours later, Raine, Detective Bolling, Peachins, and the young Roe sat silently in the office of the Prosecutor of Tarrytown. Someone had called to dispatch a car to take the body to the morgue in Tarrytown. Apart from the usual procedures of collecting the remains and their belongings, Bolling ordered no one to touch the body. The officers searched for fragments of the clothing, hoping to find buttons that could provide clues to the victim's identity. However, the explosion had damaged the body so much that the officer soon gave up searching. The explosives expert happily said that the body was not turned into atoms, a miracle. [2198] They sat at the prosecutor's desk, staring at the items on the table. This was the only item taken from under the body that could be used as a clue \u2013 a cheap British-made watch with a leather strap. If traced back to its source, it would be a waste of time. The watch case was almost gone, leaving only a small triangular piece of glass. The synthetic metal used to make the watch, despite its surface turning gray-black, was not damaged by the explosion. There was one strange thing: the hands pointed to 12:26; the face of the watch had a deep cut that went over the number \"10\" and extended to the edge of the watch. [2199] \"That's odd,\" the prosecutor, a young man with worried eyes, said. \"Bolling, didn't you tell me the body was found face down, with the arm wearing this watch pressed under the stomach?\" [2200] \"Yes.\" [2201] \"Then the cut on the case of the watch was not left by the explosion.\" [2202] \"There are other suspicious things,\" Percy said. \"If the watch stopped because of the explosion, the pointer should have stopped at six o'clock, but it didn't.\" [2203] The prosecutor looked at her approvingly. \"Right! I didn't even think of it myself. You're Sam Chief's daughter, aren't you?\" [2204] The coroner rushed in - a short, bald man with a pink face and a soft chin - \"Hello, hello! I just finished checking the mess inside the body.\" \"He was murdered, right?\" Rowe asked eagerly. [2205] \"Yes.\" [2206] \"Yes, indeed. Of course, it's difficult to make a definite diagnosis based on the condition of the body, but I estimate that he died more than thirty-six hours ago, so he must have died around midnight on Sunday.\" [2207] \"Midnight on Sunday!\" Percy glared at Roe, who glared back at her. Mr. Jury Ring shuddered. [2208] \"This is consistent with the time on the watch,\" the prosecutor concluded. \"Twelve o'clock - twenty - six minutes. The watch must have stopped at the time of the murder. He was killed at zero point twenty-six on Monday.\" [2209] The little bald man continued, \"He was shot from the front, at close range. Three bullets.\" He dropped three deformed bullets on the table. \"The slash on the case is strange. There is also a deep cut on the wrist. The beginning of the cut on the wrist is exactly where the slash on the case stops.\" [2210] \"In other words,\" Roe asked, \"you think the same attack caused the cuts on both the wrist and the case?\" [2211] \"That's the standard answer!\" [2212] \"This is our axe-wielder,\" Roe's eyes blazed with anger, \"or at least the person who used the axe... Doctor, could these cuts have been made by an axe?\" [2213] \"Yes, it couldn't have been a knife. The blade of the weapon must have been wide and had a handle.\" [2214] \"That settles it,\" Pauling said. \"Someone used an axe on this guy, hitting his wrist, smashing his watch, stopping it, and injuring his wrist. And, I think, the killer fed him lead pellets.\" [2215] \"There's one more thing.\" The coroner pulled a small key wrapped in a tissue from his pocket. \"Pauling, your men just brought it over, they found it in a fragment of a pants pocket near the body. Someone has identified it\u2014\"\n[2216] \"Maxwell?\" [2217] \"The caretaker of the house? Yes, Maxwell identified this as the original key to the front door.\" [2218] \"Original!\" The two young men exclaimed in unison. [2219] \"Strange.\" Pauling said. \"Wait a minute!\" He picked up the prosecutor's phone and dialed the headquarters in Tarrytown. He spoke briefly to someone and then hung up the phone, saying, \"Yes. My men told me this is Dr. Alessi's key. The key taken by the masked man who tied Maxwell up in the garage that night was a copy.\" [2220] \"The only original?\" Percy gasped. [2221] \"That's what Maxwell said.\" [2222] \"So, there's nothing to doubt,\" the prosecutor sighed contentedly. \"The body must be that of Dr. Allais.\" [2223] \"Really?\" Reine murmured. [2224] \"Don't you think so?\" [2225] \"Dear sir, a key does not necessarily represent an owner. However, I suppose that logically, it makes sense.\" [2226] \"Well, I'm busy.\" The forensic expert said, \"And there's one more thing. I think you need the deceased's physical features. He's five feet eleven inches tall, has light yellow or golden hair, weighs about one hundred fifty-five pounds, and is between forty-five and fifty-five years old. I couldn't find any other identifying marks.\" [2227] \"Sedra.\" Percy whispered. [2228] \"Bingo.\" Roe impatiently said, \"A British doctor named Sedra was involved in this case, and he disappeared from his New York hotel on Saturday. This description fits him perfectly.\" [2229] \"You don't mean that guy!\" Paul shouted. [2230] \"I do mean him. However, the identification seems a bit vague. This Sedra person is also suspected to be Dr. Alessi\u2014\"\n[2231] \"So that's it!\" Bauer exclaimed hopefully. \"Don't forget, we carried the body with Dr. Aless' key. If Sedra is Aless, then everything falls into place.\" [2232] \"I'm not so sure,\" Rowe stammered. \"There are actually two possibilities. We're arguing over here because we haven't analyzed it thoroughly enough. The first possibility is that Sedra and Aless are the same person, just like you said, Mr. Bauer. In this case, the body \u2013 the features match both of them \u2013 clears up the mystery of the two missing persons. But if Sedra and Aless are not the same person, then we can only conclude that they are 'physically similar.' We've been avoiding this conclusion because it seems \u2013 uh \u2013 like a clich\u00e9 from a novel, but you can't ignore it.\" [2233] Reen remained silent. [2234] \"Well,\" Bauer stood up, \"maybe these ramblings can solve your problem. I just want to know one thing: who is this corpse? Dr. Aless? Or that damn Britisher Sedra?\" [2235] Two important things happened on Tuesday morning. Chief Sam returned victorious from Ohio, having apprehended the jewel thief who was safely behind bars in an iron window; and the mystery of the \"physically similar\" was solved. [2236] Chapter 26\n[2237] Resurrection\n[2238] The next morning, the two old men and two young people sat under the large oak tree in the peaceful, serene garden of Reine. \"We're here again because...\" the officer cheerfully told Reine, \"Petty told me she and this young man have practically lived here! We have some interesting news to tell you.\" [2239] \"News?\" the old gentleman shrugged, looking tired and weary, then he smiled weakly, a little of his old-time pleasantness creeping into his voice, \"\u2018Fill me with your rich voice, and let all my senses be fed, / Till I am as a seagull come to cormorant's nest on the head.\u2019 (1) I believe the harvest is bountiful.\" [2240] The officer grinned, in a very good mood. \"Judge for yourself!\" he reached into his pocket and pulled out an envelope, \"This morning I received an unexpected telegram from the old man Truncus.\" [2241] The telegram read:\n[2242] Further investigation into Hamnet Sedley reveals an interesting fact. The previous telegram informed you that Hamnet's brother William was missing, and it has now been discovered that William and Hamnet are twin brothers. William has arrived in America, arriving in New York from Bordeaux in late March. He is wanted by the Bordeaux police for illegal entry and malicious destruction of the private library of a French book collector, with the intent to steal rare books. The Frenchman was brutally beaten after discovering that William had damaged the cover of a 1599 edition of Shakespeare's \"The Passionate Pilgrim.\" William's behavior is strange, and he is well-off, like Hamnet, a book collector, and disappeared from Britain three years ago under the pseudonym of Dr. Allis. He served as an expert in rare book auctions, purchasing books for millionaire collectors. His best customer was the recently deceased Sir Humphrey. Both William and Hamnet have no fingerprints on record, and they have no discernible features to consider, but they bear a striking resemblance to each other.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIf you have any information on the whereabouts of William Sedley or Dr. Allis, please inform the Bordeaux police. Best regards, hunting successfully. [2243] Trunch\n[2244] \"This explains everything, you see,\" Percy exclaimed, \"As twins, Hamnet and William must be as similar as peanuts in a shell, which is why everyone got them confused.\"\" [2245] \"Yes,\" Raine said softly, \"This news is invaluable. Obviously, Sedley is Hamnet Sedley, Dr. Allis is Sedley's brother William, the fugitive wanted by the French police.\" He interlaced his long fingers, \"But the problem still remains the same. Which corpse was discovered? Hamnet or William?\" [2246] Rowe said: \"And William's attempt to steal the 1599 Jagger in Brie. Mr. Reine, have you heard of this Frenchman Pierre Girouard? In fact, I visited him last year.\" - Reine nodded - \"He is the owner of the second rare book. Saxony is the third, and the other one is unknown. Damaged cover, huh? Nonsense, he was looking for the Shakespeare document!\" [2247] \"Understood, boy?\" the sergeant smiled, \"I'm out of this case now. But we're making some progress, aren't we?\" [2248] \"You want to know -\" Percy suddenly spoke, his fingers idly smoothing his clothes, \"who murdered the man in the cellar?\" They were all surprised, and Percy laughed, \"Oh, I don't know the name. It's like solving a geometry problem, a bunch of unknowns. But there's one thing I'm sure of: the killer is the axe-wielder!\" [2249] \"Oh?\" Rowe rolled back onto the grass. [2250] \"From the evidence provided by the grandfather clock, he was in the study at midnight. At 12:24 he was in the bedroom upstairs - the clock in the bedroom is the evidence. The murder took place at 12:26 - just two minutes later! The killer was swinging an axe, and the deep cuts on the victim's wrist and the watch provided evidence. These can prove it.\" [2251] \"I see,\" said Lane, looking up at the blue sky. [2252] \"Isn't that right?\" Peachins asked impatiently. [2253] But Rene did not look at her lips; he seemed to be studying a strange white cloud in the sky. [2254] \"There's one more thing,\" Roe clearly said, \"The single lens glasses we found in the hallway of the house prove that Dr. Sedra was definitely inside. Was he the killer or the victim? We could easily say he was the victim because the body matches his features\u2026\"\n[2255] \"Unless the corpse is Dr. Aleas.\" Peachings said. [2256] \"But who set the bomb?\" asked the sergeant. [2257] Quincy darted ahead of a man in uniform with a brown face. [2258] \"Are you Sergeant Sam?\" asked the stranger. [2259] \"Yes.\" [2260] \"I'm Officer Bolling from the town of Thayer,\" said the stranger. [2261] \"Oh, right. I called him this morning and told him I was back.\" [2262] \"He told me they found a guy on the road between Ewington and Thayer, kind of simple, looked like he was about to starve to death. A hobo, a little crazy. Wouldn't give his name, just babbling about a blue hat.\" [2263] \"Blue hat!\" [2264] \"That's right. They took him to the Thayer hospital. The chief said you should go see him right away.\" [2265] They saw Bowlin pacing in the hospital's waiting room. He shook Sam's hand warmly: \"It's been a while, Sergeant. Damn, things just keep getting worse. Want to see him?\" [2266] \"Of course I do! Who is it?\" [2267] \"I'm not sure. They just finished taking care of him. He's a sturdy old guy, but he's so skinny you can see his ribs, and it's because he's starving.\" [2268] They followed Pauling down the hallway, getting more and more excited. [2269] Pauling opened the door to a private room. A middle-aged man lay motionless on the bed, with a pile of dirty, ragged clothes on the chair beside him. His face was gaunt and lined with deep wrinkles, his beard unkempt, and his eyes fixed on the wall. [2270] Chief Sam's jaw dropped: \"Donoher!\" [2271] \"Is this the missing Irishman?\" Pauling asked anxiously. (Is this the missing Irishman?\") [2272] Mr. Jules Renet gently closed the door and walked to the bedside, bending down to look at the Irishman. The Irishman's eyes suddenly filled with pain, and he slowly turned his head, staring blankly at Renet's gaze, then at the face of the chief of police, and then his eyes brightened. He licked his lips and whispered, \"Chief.\" (Mr. Jules Renet gently closed the door and walked to the bedside, bending down to look at the Irishman. The Irishman's eyes suddenly filled with pain, and he slowly turned his head, staring blankly at Renet's gaze, then at the face of the chief of police, and then his eyes brightened. He licked his lips and whispered, \"Chief.\") [2273] \"Hello,\" Sam said cheerfully, walking to the bedside, \"you old grouch, you've made us run around in circles. Where have you been? What happened?\" (Hello, you old grouch, you've made us run around in circles. Where have you been? What happened?\") [2274] Donohue's pale face flushed with a hint of red, and he coughed before speaking. \"Well, it's a long story,\" he tried to smile, \"they've been feeding me through a rotten tube. I'd rather trade an arm for a bloody steak. You \u2013 how did you find me, sir?\" (Well, it's a long story,\" he tried to smile, \"they've been feeding me through a rotten tube. I'd rather trade an arm for a bloody steak. You \u2013 how did you find me, sir?\") [2275] \"Donohue, we've been looking for you since you disappeared. Do you have the strength to talk?\" (Do you have the strength to talk?\") [2276] \"Of course, it's an honor,\" Donohue stroked his scruffy face and spoke with a gradually recovering voice. \"Just as I was visiting the British Museum with the Indiana delegation, I noticed a tall, thin man with a mustache and a peculiar blue hat sneaking out of the museum, with something under his arm - it looked like a book. As a man particularly alert to thieves, I didn't have time to sound the alarm and chased after him. He jumped into a taxi, and I followed. We changed various means of transportation, leaving the city and arriving at a dilapidated wooden house near the main road between Therry and Evenston. After a man in black left the house, I ran to the porch. There was a nameplate under the doorbell that read 'Dr. Allis.' I pressed the doorbell, and the man came to answer the door himself. Although he took off his hat and gray fake mustache, Donohue still recognized him. The mustache was a disguise! Donohue was at a loss because he had no evidence that the man was a thief and might have been just his imagination. However, the mustache was definitely gone... He did not have the authority to arrest him, but he accepted a cordial invitation to enter the house and was taken to the study. Donohue mustered his courage and accused the owner of stealing a book from the museum. [2277] \"He was bold enough,\" Donohue's eyes sparkled, \"to admit his guilt. Then he said he would make up for the loss, pay for it, and so on. I took out my pipe and started smoking, thinking I might as well wait until I had the chance to call the police and have him sent to the nearest police station. But I was too nervous and accidentally dropped my pipe on the ground, startling him. So he asked me to leave, and that was it. I walked down the alley, thinking hard, when suddenly my head was hit hard. That's all I know.\" [2278] When he woke up, he found himself tied up in a dark room with a cloth stuffed in his mouth. He had initially thought that Dr. Allis had followed him and attacked him; he had made this assumption until today. He had discovered that the prison that held him was not Dr. Allis's house but a place he had never seen before. [2279] \"Are you sure?\" the inspector said softly. \"Certainly. Dr. Allis's house is destroyed. Continue, Donohue.\" [2280] \"I don't know how long I was unconscious like a dead pig,\" the Irishman's color returned, looking more comfortable. \"What day is it today? Oh, it doesn't matter. Every day, a man with a gun and a mask comes to feed me once.\" [2281] \"Was it Dr. Aless?\" Percy asked loudly. [2282] \"Miss, I don't know. The lighting was poor, and his voice sounded somewhat like... speaking like an Englishman, yes, that's it. I recognize the accent. I've seen many like him back home. Damn him, he always threatened to torture me, curse him!\" [2283] \"Torture?\" Percy asked in surprise. [2284] \"Miss, it was pretty much that. He talked a lot but never acted. He wanted me to tell him 'where the files were,' \" Donohue chuckled, \"I said, 'You're crazy!' He threatened me even more. I don't know what he meant by 'files,' do you understand?\" [2285] \"Strange,\" Rovery said. [2286] \"There were days he didn't even feed me,\" Donohue complained, \"Damn it, I need a leg of lamb to make up for it!\" He licked his lips and continued with the strange tale. Once \u2013 a long time ago, he said, although he couldn't remember the exact date or time, he couldn't keep track of time anymore \u2013 he heard a sound coming from somewhere in the house, a heavy body being dragged on the ground, obviously someone was thrown into a nearby room. Then he heard someone groaning in pain. After a while, he heard the door slam shut. He wanted to signal his neighbor to communicate, as he believed they were both in trouble, but he was tied up and his mouth was gagged, so all his efforts were in vain. For three days, Donohue hadn't eaten and hadn't seen the person who had captured him. After days