and the front door quickly gave way like a rotten nutshell. They rushed into the house, drawing their guns. The house was empty and filthy, with no furniture except for the room where Donohue had been held captive. They kicked open one door after another, finally entering a dark and stinking little room with an iron bed, a washbasin, and a chair. A person was tied up on the bed. [2296] He was unconscious. [2297] Paulin's men carried him out into the sunlight, all staring at the twisted, yellowed face of the victim. The same question appeared in everyone's eyes: Was this the starving, filthy victim, Hamnet or William Sedley? The only certainty was that he was one of the two. [2298] Donohue finished his task, emitting a weak groan and collapsing into the arms of the constable. An ambulance that had been following them sped up and took Donohue away. An intern doctor bent over to examine the unconscious Britisher's feeble limbs. [2299] \"He just fainted. Too tight, lack of food, poor air - ordinary signs of weakness. He'll be fine with care.\" [2300] The newly rescued man had a thin, sunken face with delicate golden stubble on his cheeks. The young doctor administered a nutrient injection, and the man's eyes opened, but his gaze was vacant, and he looked dazedly at the constable's shouted questions before closing his eyes again. [2301] The ambulance drove off, and a car pulled up. A young man without a hat jumped out, later to be identified as a reporter drawn to the scene by the popular mystery rumors. Pauling and Sam were stunned by the questions and answered them all. Despite Rayne's frantic winks and gestures, the information was leaked: all about Dr. Allees, \"the French fugitive being pursued by the police,\" Donohue's harrowing story, the confusion over the identities of the Suddle twins...\n[2302] The young man left with a triumphant smile. Rayne coldly said, \"Constable, that was a mistake.\" [2303] Sam blushed. At that moment, a man walked up to Pauling and reported that despite a thorough search, there was no evidence linking the kidnapper to the house. \"I also called the owner of the house in Tirry Town,\" he said. \"He didn't know anyone was living here. He said the house had been 'empty' for three years.\" [2304] He reported that \"I also called the owner of the house in Tirry Town,\" he said. \"He didn't know anyone was living here. He said the house had been 'empty' for three years.\" [2305] He reported that \"I also called the owner of the house in Tirry Town,\" he said. \"He didn't know anyone was living here. He said the house had been 'empty' for three years.\" [2306] The two teams sat silently in their own cars for ten minutes before Gordon Rowe finally said, \"Let's talk about the mystery.\" [2307] \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n[2308] (1) From Shakespeare's play \"Antony and Cleopatra\"\n[2309] Chapter 27\n[2310] Three hundred years of crime\n[2311] Inspector Sam's face was grim. \"The first thing we need to figure out is \u2013 who is he?\" [2312] They arrived at the Teri Town Hospital the next morning, gathering around the Britisher's bed. The chief doctor had called earlier to say that the patient could speak, and careful care, sedatives, and a good night's sleep had worked wonders on him. He had shaved, and there was a glint of animation in his dull face, and his eyes were distant and intelligent. They saw him sitting up in bed, with morning papers spread out on the quilt, cheerfully chatting with the man in the next bed. [2313] The Britisher raised his pale eyebrows. \"What's the question? I don't understand your meaning.\" He looked them over shrewdly, one by one, as if weighing them with a secret scale. His voice was weak, but familiar in tone. [2314] \"I am Dr. Hamnett Sedra.\" [2315] \"Ah!\" said Lane, \"That's great news for Joate!\" [2316] \"Dr. Jotter? Oh, right, Dr. Jotter! He must be worried. Donohue thought I was the man he was tracking, the man in the blue cap. Ha, ha! We look so alike - it's amazing.\u201d He composed himself, \"You know, he's my twin brother.\" [2317] \"So you know he's dead?\" Percy exclaimed. Reen glanced at the detective, who was turning red. [2318] \"I've been surrounded by reporters all morning. Then these newspapers told me everything. Based on the forensic description of the body, it must have been my brother William. You know, he always used a pseudonym, Dr. Alessi.\" [2319] \"Well,\" Sam said, \"Listen, Dr. Sedlar, it seems the case is finally solved. But what the answer is, I know for sure. We've told you - you've got some things that don't add up, and now it's about your brother. We need to know the truth. Since your brother is dead, there's no need to keep it to yourself.\" [2320] Dr. Sedlar sighed. \"I suppose you're right! Well, I'll tell you everything.\" He closed his eyes, his voice barely audible, \"You've made a big fuss about you not telling us when you arrived in New York. I secretly came first to correct a disgraceful behavior of my brother William.\" He paused, and no one spoke; he opened his eyes, \"There are too many people here.\" He suddenly said. [2321] \"Oh, come on, doctor,\" Rowe said, \"we're all in this together, and as for Donohue\u2026\"\n[2322] \"I'm deaf, dumb, and blind,\" the Irishman said with a smile. [2323] He told the story reluctantly.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThe entire room was devastated during the search, with all furniture overturned, glass items shattered, and wooden wall panels showing signs of being hacked by sharp objects. [1984] Officer Pauling quickly found the weapon, a small axe lying on the floor near the fireplace. [1985] \"That's our axe,\" Maxwell licked his lips again. \"I took it from the kitchen toolbox. I used it to chop wood for the fireplace.\" [1986] \"Is that the only axe here?\" Percy asked. [1987] \"Yes, ma'am.\" [1988] Wooden items and wooden walls had been attacked relentlessly, with wooden fragments scattered everywhere. Even the floor had one spot pryed open, where Maxwell said there had been a carpet. The carpet was now lying in a corner, as if it had been kicked there. Another corner had once held a magnificent Victorian-style floor clock, now lying on the ground, surrounded by glass fragments. The examination revealed that the person wielding the axe had deliberately smashed the cabinet, pulled off the brass pendulum, flipped the clock over, and hacked the back and sides, exposing the delicate mechanism. The clock's hands pointed exactly at twelve. [1989] \"Was this clock still working last night?\" Rowe asked sharply. [1990] \"Yes, sir. I... I was playing solitaire here when the doorbell rang, so I know. The clock was ticking, it was working.\" [1991] \"So he broke this clock in the middle of the night,\" Pechen murmured. \"That might be useful.\" [1992] \"I don't see how,\" Pauling said. \"We know from Maxwell's story that he came at eleven-thirty, didn't he?\" [1993] Jules Renet stood aside, deep in thought, his eyes alert and flashing. [1994] Pechen slowly walked around the room. She checked the desk, with all the drawers pulled out and their contents scattered on the floor, including scattered playing cards. Then she noticed something across the room and squinted. It was a cheap alarm clock sitting on the mantelpiece. [1995] \"Patti, what's wrong?\" Rowley noticed her intense expression. [1996] \"That alarm clock. It's strange to have an alarm clock in the study.\" She walked over to pick it up, and the clock cheerfully ticked away. [1997] \"That's mine, miss.\" Maxwell apologized, seeming to have recovered from the shock, curiously watching the unfolding events. [1998] \"Why do you need a small alarm clock when there's a big grandfather clock in the room?\" Percy suspiciously asked. [1999] \"Oh, about this alarm clock...\" Maxwell hurried over to answer, \"Miss, I've been a bit sick these past few days. On Saturday, I bought some cough medicine in Tarrytown, and the pharmacist told me to take a teaspoonful every four hours. Last night, I took one at eight o'clock, but I'm a bit forgetful, miss. I thought I might forget to take it before going to bed, so I brought the alarm clock in while playing cards, set the alarm for midnight to remind myself to take it first, and then go to sleep. But when I \u2013\"\n[2000] \"So that's it.\" Percy said. The story seemed to have no suspicious aspects, as there was a brown medicine bottle next to the alarm clock on the mantelpiece, about three-quarters full, and a sticky spoon. She checked the alarm clock and found that it was indeed set to twelve o'clock, with the timing rod pushed to the \"on\" position. \"I was thinking\u2026\" she whispered, then looked at her own small watch, which showed the time as eleven fifty-one. \"Gordon, what time is it on your watch?\" [2001] \"About eleven fifty-five.\" [2002] \"Mr. Balling, do you have a watch?\" [2003] \"Eleven fifty-two.\" Balling is unconcerned. \"What's this about?\" [2004] \"I was just wondering how accurate this alarm clock is.\" Percyns smiles slightly, but her eyes are puzzled. \"You see, it's very accurate.\" Indeed, the cheap clock's hands point to eleven fifty-one. [2005] \"Ah, Percyns!\" Laine murmurs, approaching, \"May I take a look?\" He roughly checks it, puts the clock back on the mantelpiece, and returns to his corner. [2006] \"What the hell is that?\" Roeve asked, puzzled, as he wandered around the ruins, flipping through things. He tilted his head back, gazing at the top of a wall. This wall's paneling was different from the others, the bookshelf embedded into the wall from floor to ceiling, while the others only went halfway up. A sliding ladder, similar to those used in shoe stores or libraries, could move along the metal rail track at the bottom of the wall, obviously added by the previous owner to easily reach books that were otherwise out of reach. Above the top shelf, there was a row of cherrywood panels, similar to the ones on the other three walls. They were narrower, seeming like something from an older generation. What caught Roeve's attention was one of the panels, which had been lifted from the wall, acting like a door. [2007] \"Well, looks like a secret cabinet,\" the young man chuckled. \"I hope the next minute the Count of Monte Cristo jumps out of the fireplace.\" He nimbly climbed up the ladder, which was just below the open panel near the ceiling. [2008] \"What in the world is this?\" Bowling grumbled, irritated. \"A secret cabinet! Sounds like something out of a detective novel\u2026 Maxwell, do you know about this?\" [2009] The old man gaped upwards. \"No, sir. I've never seen this before. Oh, this door is quite small.\" [2010] \"Empty!\" Roeve announced with displeasure. \"A perfect place to hide things! Probably\u2014wait\u2014eight inches wide, two inches high, two inches deep\u2026 It must have been done by Dr. Aless. Quite clever. This was recently completed, and the marks from the chisel are still fresh.\" They watched him as he looked around, \"The person who destroyed this place was quite unfortunate. He didn't find this hole.\" Then he pointed to the narrow panel above the top shelf, where the wood had been hacked at viciously with an axe. However, when Roeve closed the small door, there were no marks left. \"What a clever trick! Quite clever. Now, how do we open this thing again?\" [2011] \"Let me take a look, young man,\" Pauling said worriedly. [2012] Roebling reluctantly descended, and the policeman carefully climbed up. As Roebling had said, the secret cabinet was made very skillfully. Now that the door was closed, there were no obvious traces. The gap was very fine, just at the edge of the panel, and it was difficult to notice. Pauling pulled and tugged, but the door remained closed, and the panel's exterior still looked ordinary, although he could hear a hollow sound when he tapped it with his fingers. The frame of the panel was the same as the others, with small wooden roses inlaid. Pauling panted, \"There must be some trick to it.\" He began to touch the small roses, then exclaimed. One of them turned in his fingers. He turned it once, and it didn't work. He turned it again, and the door suddenly jumped open, and he almost fell off the ladder...he removed the door and examined the interior. The primitive spring mechanism was very clever. [2013] \"Alright,\" he descended to the floor. \"Don't worry about this. Whatever was inside...if there was anything, it's already gone. The gap is really small. Let's go upstairs.\" [2014] Dr. Aless's bedroom was hacked apart just like the downstairs study. The bed was completely disassembled, the mattress was torn to shreds, the furniture was shattered, and the floor was lifted up\u2014clearly the person with the axe had continued to search in Dr. Aless's room. There was a small gilded clock in the room. Curiously, it too had escaped the hurricane-like destruction, falling from the table to the floor. The hands stopped at 12:24. [2015] Pecsin's eyes lit up. \"Our friend left a time schedule for his activities. This proves that he first attacked downstairs... Maxwell, does this clock keep good time?\" [2016] \"Yes, miss. All the clocks are accurate, although they're cheap. And I often wind them up to keep them all in sync.\" [2017] \"That's lucky,\" Raine murmured. \"The man is so stupid!\" [2018] \"What?\" Pauling exclaimed sharply. [2019] \"Oh, nothing, Mr. Pauling. I was just criticizing the stupidity of criminals in general.\" [2020] A baritone voice called up the stairs, \"Hey! Look what I found!\" [2021] They rushed down the stairs. A policeman stood in the hallway, holding a flashlight over a dark, dirty corner. Under the light, they saw three shards of glass, one of which was tied with a black long silk ribbon. [2022] Rene picked up the glass shard and brought it into the living room. He put the three shards together, and they formed a perfect circular piece of glass. [2023] \"A single lens,\" he said quietly. [2024] \"Oh my!\" Rowe whispered. [2025] \"A single lens?\" Maxwell blinked. \"Sir, that's quite strange. Dr. Al\u00e9s doesn't wear glasses. I've never seen one in the house, and I\u2014\"\n[2026] \"Dr. Sedra.\" Percy worriedly said. [2027] Chapter 23\n[2028] Symbolic Issue\n[2029] There was nothing to do at the scene. Maxwell accepted the advice and left the house, returning to his peaceful life in Tarrytown, leaving the energetic and hardworking Mr. Bowling to monitor the house, leaving two men to guard the alley and the back of the house - although you could not enter from the back, you would have to flatten the lush shrubs and the fallen leaves piled up high. [2030] Bowling took Maxwell in the police car to Tarrytown. At the invitation of the elderly gentleman, Percy and Rowley drove in the Dromio's car to Hamlet Manor. The young people, grateful for the help of the busy and hardworking Mr. Foster, retreated to their rooms to rest, washed and came downstairs to have a late lunch, although they might not fill their spirits, they could definitely fill their stomachs. During the meal, no one spoke much, Percy was unusually silent, Rowley was full of thoughts, and Rene said some trivial things without mentioning the morning's events. After lunch, he handed the guests over to Quincy and retired to the study. [2031] After lunch, he handed the guests over to Quincey and retired to the study himself. Percy and Rowe wandered around the spacious Hamlet Manor. They strolled through the charming garden and lay comfortably on the quiet grass. Quincey saw them, chuckled, and disappeared. [2032] Birds chirped, and the grass smelled sweet and fragrant. Both were silent. Rowe turned to look at his companion's face. In the warm sunlight, her cheeks were rosy, and her slender figure was relaxed and graceful. He looked at her warmly, feeling strange; she was both captivating and unattainable. Her eyes were closed, and a fine line appeared between her eyebrows, refusing to joke or flirt. [2033] Rowe sighed. \"Patty, what are you thinking? Please don't frown like that! I like my woman to be ordinary.\" [2034] She murmured, \"Am I frowning?\" Then she opened her eyes and smiled at him. \"Gordon, you're a child. I've been thinking \u2013\"\n[2035] \"I think I have to marry a wife with a sharp mind,\" the young man said lightly. \"The point is, I'm sharp-minded too \u2013 so there would be two of us\u2026\"\n[2036] \"Oh\u2014\" Rowe suddenly lay back on the grass, plucking a blade of grass. [2037] She sat up, her eyes gentle. \"So you figured it out too, Gordon? One of them was the axe-wielder. The state of the house showed that he was looking for something; he didn't know where it was, and he was determined to find it. He was systematically destroying furniture and other things with an axe. Most importantly, this person was not Dr. Ales.\" [2038] Rowe yawned. \"Of course not. If he was Ales, he would have known where to find his own things. The secret cabinet on the wall was definitely his doing.\" [2039] \"Don't be so uninterested, please?\" Percy smiled. \"You're almost tearing your brain apart... I don't know. Your reasoning is correct. The axe-wielder is our unknown variable, and Dr. Ales wouldn't burn down his own place to make firewood, and he knows where the axe-wielder's thing is. But on the other hand, the axe-wielder's thing was found\u2014evidence that the secret cabinet was open, so someone must have opened it.\" [2040] \"So you think there were two people in the house last night? The axe-wielder\u2014such a melodramatic term\u2014why couldn't he find the cabinet himself? He put so much effort into doing something so despicable.\" [2041] \"Alright, you're being clever