ere.\" [1840] The study was spacious, with oak boards lining the floor and ceiling, followed by rows of bookshelves. However, most of the shelves were empty, with only a few sparse books at the bottom. [1841] \"From the appearance of his study,\" Rowe commented, \"he's using it as a temporary hiding place.\" [1842] \"Indeed it seems so,\" Reane agreed. [1843] The ceiling was low, and an ancient, ugly stained-glass chandelier hung from the center, just above the dilapidated desk. The farthest wall had a fireplace with a mantel made of a single block of oak. The blackened grate had burnt-out wood and ashes. An old quill pen, a pot of ink, a reading magnifying glass, and some miscellaneous items were on the desk. [1844] The inspector and Percy exclaimed together, slamming their hands on the table. [1845] \"What is it?\" Rowe shouted, rushing forward. [1846] A cigarette ashtray lay on the table\u2014a cracked, ugly piece of colored ceramic, with several pitifully small dolphins supporting a bulbous mermaid. Five pale gray shards lay in the center of the ashtray, two of them concave and bearing burn marks. The shards were surrounded by ash and tobacco. [1847] \"It looks like a cheap clay pipe,\" Rowe said in confusion. \"What's so surprising about that?\" [1848] \"Donovan!\" the sergeant said, naming a person. [1849] Percy's blue eyes shone as he exclaimed, \"That's the evidence! Gordon, Donovan always uses clay pipes. He must have followed Dr. Aless here from the museum that day. It proves he was here.\" [1850] \"Maxwell!\" the sergeant barked. \"You said no ugly Irishmen had come to this house recently. How did the pipe get here?\" [1851] \"I don't know, sir. I haven't been in this room since Dr. Aless left. I picked up these fragments when I went to send the parcel out that morning before leaving. I placed them on top of the ash and tobacco in the ashtray.\" [1852] Reine sighed. \"Did you notice these fragments when Dr. Aless sent you away that evening?\" [1853] \"They weren't there when I left. I'm certain.\" [1854] \"Does Dr. Aless smoke a clay pipe?\" [1855] \"Dr. Aless doesn't smoke at all. We found this ashtray in the shed when we arrived.\" Maxwell blinked. \"I don't smoke either.\" He declared solemnly. [1856] \"I think, Inspector -\" the old gentleman was tired, \"we can connect the dots to some extent. We know that Dr. Allison sent Maxwell away on the 27th evening, and that he was followed from the city to here and waited in the bushes outside the house. We can confirm that. What happened next is only a matter of speculation.\" [1857] \"This is interesting.\" Sam frowned. \"Let's check the other rooms.\" [1858] They climbed the creaky stairs. The upstairs corridor was narrow and had several doors. They checked each room one by one. Two were empty and covered in spider webs, clearly Maxwell was not a diligent caretaker. One was Maxwell's room, with nothing but an iron bed, an old-fashioned washbasin, a chair, and a five-drawer chest found in the basement of some junk dealer. [1859] Perkins examined the sheets with a woman's eye and asked sternly, \"Did you make this?\" [1860] \"Yes, Miss,\" Maxwell swallowed, \"the last time was the morning of the 27th.\" [1861] \"Really?\" Reane murmured, \"How could that be? You came back on the morning of the twenty-eighth and found Dr. Alais had left, the package was on the corridor downstairs, and you didn't see any signs of someone sleeping in the bed?\" [1862] \"No, sir. That's why I knew Dr. Alais must have left the night before, the night he sent me back to Thirlby. Because on Tuesday morning, I found he hadn't slept in the bed.\" [1863] \"Why didn't you say that earlier?\" Sam scolded, \"This is important. Whatever happened on that Monday night, it must have happened before Alais appeared, I mean - before Sedra appeared.\" [1864] \"All right, Inspector,\" the elderly gentleman smiled, \"let's not be too hard on him. Let's just call the missing guest Dr. Alais for now - Dr. Alais. He has a peculiar name, doesn't he? Don't you think so?\" [1865] Gordon Rowe, who had been rummaging through the wardrobe, straightened up and spoke up assertively, \"I do find it peculiar. If this treacherous world has any sense or order, this strange name proves the Inspector right and you wrong.\" [1866] \"Ah, Gordon,\" Rene's smile remained odd, \"I knew things wouldn't escape your hound-like observational skills.\" [1867] \"What does that mean?\" Percy exclaimed. [1868] \"What?\" the inspector barked, turning red with frustration. [1869] Joe Vera sat down in the chair, looking disgusted, as if bored to tears by these madmen. As for Maxwell, he stood with his mouth half-open, staring at them like an idiot. [1870] \"In fact\u2026\" Rowe spoke quickly, \"Dr. Aless's name has six very unusual letters. Think about it.\" [1871] \"Letters?\" Pechinse repeated, puzzled. \"A-l-e-s... Oh, Gordon, I'm so stupid!\" [1872] \"What?\" the chief of police echoed. \"A-1-e-s...\"\n[1873] \"Not A-l-e-s,\" Raine said. \"It's D-r-a-l-e-s.\"\n[1874] Rowe gave Raine a strange look. \"So you figured it out too! Pechinse, didn't you realize that Dr. Ales's name formed a very intriguing word puzzle?\" [1875] Pechinse's eyes widened, and his face paled, as he uttered a name. [1876] \"Exactly. Rearrange the letters in Dr. Ales and you get\u2026 Sedlar!\" [1877] \"Indeed,\" the elderly gentleman murmured. [1878] \u2026\n[1879] There was a moment of silence, and then Rowe quietly turned his attention back to the wardrobe. [1880] \"Hey!\" Sam the sergeant exclaimed loudly, \"Boy, you're not as dumb as you look! Reen, you can't deny it now!\" [1881] \"Maybe not,\" Rayne smiled, \"I agree with Gordon. The Dr. Ales anagram is too clever to be a coincidence; it's been planned. But what kind of plan? Where from? What for...?\" He shrugged, \"Since I started exploring the bizarre states of the human mind, I've learned not to jump to conclusions.\" [1882] \"I'm ready to make my conclusion,\" the sergeant said sternly, just as Rowe let out a satisfied growl. [1883] Rowe crawled out of the wardrobe, muttering to himself, and then quickly turned around, placing his uninjured hand behind his back. [1884] He grinned, \"Guess what I found. That old Ales, what a jerk and a sly one!\" [1885] \"Gordon, what did you find?\" Percy asked, hurrying over to him. [1886] He gestured with his bandaged arm for her not to come closer. \"Wait, miss, mind your reputation.\" He suddenly lost his smile, \"Mr. Rene, you must be interested in this.\" [1887] Before everyone recovered from their shock, Rowe turned and delved into the wardrobe again. [1888] He pulled out three things in succession - a bizarrely colored blue fedora, a pair of blue glasses, and an exaggerated silver-grey handlebar mustache. [1889] \"Today is my lucky day!\" the young man chuckled, \"Look at these treasures!\" [1890] \"Damn it,\" the inspector said blankly, watching Rowe's admiring expression with a mix of jealousy and envy. [1891] \"Oh, Gordon.\" [1892] Reen took the false mustache, glasses, handlebar mustache, and hat from Rowe's hand and whispered, \"I think it should be fine, the mustache and glasses should be the same ones you've seen, right?\" [1893] \"Listen,\" Sam grumbled, \"there's no way there could be two of these in the world. Can you imagine a sane person wearing a mustache like this?\" [1894] \"Certainly, there could be a special case,\" Reen smiled. \"Maxwell, have you ever seen these things before?\" [1895] The butler was startled by the sight of the mustache and shook his head. \"I've never seen the mustache or the glasses, sir.\" [1896] The elderly gentleman grumbled, \"This hat... Verla, this is the hat that Dr. Alessis wore when you followed him to Britain, isn't it? And these sideburns?\" [1897] \"Yes, I told you what this guy was up to, I'm not\u2014\"\n[1898] \"The evidence is clear,\" Reine said thoughtfully, \"Inspector, without a doubt, the person who left this letter for you on May 6th and the one who stole the book from Britain on May 27th is the same person. The evidence is clear\u2014\"\n[1899] \"The evidence is clear,\" the inspector said with a savage hatred in his tone, \"This case is clear. With this evidence, along with Clara and Verla's testimony, and that photo, he has nowhere to run. Tell you, there's no Sedra in this case!\" [1900] \"No Sedra? Inspector, you scared me. What do you mean?\" [1901] \"But there's a Sedra!\" Roe argued, and Percy frowned at his father. [1902] \"I've cracked this tangled case, thank God! It's as simple as eating a pastry,\" the sergeant said. \"The man who showed up at the museum, claiming to be their new curator, Dr. Sedra, is not Sedra at all, but Dr. Alessi. Whether he is or not, I'll bet my hat that Alessi killed Sedra before he could arrive in New York and take up his position, then took his place\u2014pretending to be him, they look alike and are about the same height and build, those British people all look alike\u2014and started a series of schemes. I'll tell you, Dr. Alessi is not only a thief, but a murderer!\" [1903] \"For me, the question is: Who is Dr. Alessi?\" Roe said. [1904] \"You can easily verify your statement,\" Rains said with a flicker in his eyes. \"Just send a telegram to your friend Trunch at Scotland Yard and ask him to send you a photo of Hamnett Sedra.\" [1905] \"That's a good idea,\" Percy said. [1906] \"I'm not so sure\u2014\" Reane said. [1907] The inspector's lower lip protruded as he listened to these words, his face suddenly turning a deep purple. He waved his hands and shouted, \"I'm crazy! I get nothing out of this whole thing. I'm quitting! This case has kept me awake for days, who cares?\" [1908] \"What am I supposed to do?\" Maxwell asked helplessly. \"I have some money left from Dr. Allis, but if he doesn't come back\u2014\"\n[1909] \"Forget it, lock up the house and go home,\" Reane said. [1910] \"I don't think so,\" Mr. Reane whispered. \"No, Inspector, I don't think so. Maxwell, I think you'd better stay here and pretend nothing happened.\" [1911] \"But, sir\u2014\u2014\" Maxwell scratched his sagging cheek. [1912] \"If Dr. Allais comes back - and it's not completely impossible - I think the sergeant would be glad to hear the news.\" [1913] \"Okay, sir.\" Maxwell sighed. [1914] \"Damn it, I don't want to\u2014\u2014\" The sergeant cursed. [1915] \"Well, you old tyrant,\" Rene smiled, \"give Maxwell a business card... that's better.\" He took Sam's arm, \"Maxwell, remember to notify Dr. Allais as soon as he comes back!\" [1916] Chapter Twenty-One\n[1917] The Perils of Westchester\n[1918] Suddenly, the case seemed to come to an abrupt end, as if struck by a plague. The entire week lay dead and still, with nothing happening, no new developments, and most importantly, no one caring about what was happening. [1919] The sergeant, true to his word, let go of the case. He was fully occupied with the jewelry heist case, which had shaken society and involved a priceless pearl necklace and a socialite attacked on Park Avenue. He rarely appeared in the office, only to check his mail. Apart from Percy's occasional visits, Sam Detective Agency was left in the hands of Miss Broadie, who dealt with it with tearful sighs. [1920] As for Percy, she suddenly became passionate about learning and frequented the British Museum, where she garnered the admiration of many men. The men were still busy restoring the worn-out building and trying to give it a fresh look. She and Mr. Rowe showed diligence in their study of Shakespeare, but their collaboration did not uncover many secrets of the playwright. Apart from discussing the enigmatic Dr. Siddra and their own affairs, Mr. Rowe's studies made little progress. [1921] But the most indifferent person seemed to be Mr. Philo Vance.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."